[[:db/cas 17592186388538 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388540 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388541 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388542 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388543 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388544 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388545 :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388545 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388547 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388548 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388549 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388550 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388551 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388552 :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388552 :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388554 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388555 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388556 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388557 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388558 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388559 :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388559 :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388561 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388562 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388563 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388564 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388565 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388566 :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388566 :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388568 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388569 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388570 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388571 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388572 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388625 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388625 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=33 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5(-3) & =33 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =18 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}33 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=33 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5(-3) & =33 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =18 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388627 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388628 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388629 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388630 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388631 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388639 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388641 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388642 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388643 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388644 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388645 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388646 :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388646 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(1-2{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2+1=5.$" "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(1-2{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2+1=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388653 :question/problem "Find ${B}^{120},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[60]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[40]{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find ${B}^{120},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[60]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[40]{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388653 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $B:$\n\n$$\nB={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6