"] [:db/cas 17592186388672 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10+51\\text{i} \\\\ 24-20\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}10+51\\text{i} \\\\ 24-20\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388673 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16-27\\text{i} \\\\ 23-35\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}16-27\\text{i} \\\\ 23-35\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388674 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388674 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=3[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=3\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=2\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388676 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388677 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388678 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388679 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388680 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388681 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ find $T(v).$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ find $T(v).$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388681 :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $\\,T(v)=T\\cdot v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the fact that $\\,T(v)=T\\cdot v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388683 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388684 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388685 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388686 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}29 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}29 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388687 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388688 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388688 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388690 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388691 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388692 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388693 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388694 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388695 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388695 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388697 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388698 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388699 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388700 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ 24 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ 24 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388701 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -27 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -27 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388702 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" "$$\n[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388702 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388704 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388705 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & 36 \\\\ -25 & 14\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 36 \\\\ -25 & 14\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388706 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -25 \\\\ 20 & -36\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -25 \\\\ 20 & -36\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388707 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -36 \\\\ 20 & -25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -36 \\\\ 20 & -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388708 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -20\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -20\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388709 :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388709 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=15,$ and $c=6.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{15+6}{1}=21.$" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6,$ we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=15,$ and $c=6.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{15+6}{1}=21.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388716 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388716 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388718 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388719 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388720 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388721 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388722 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388723 :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388723 :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+7{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 11\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+7{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \