\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388732 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388733 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388734 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 10 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 10 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388735 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388736 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388737 :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388737 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388739 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388740 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388741 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388742 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388743 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388744 :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388744 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388746 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388747 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388748 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388749 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388750 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388778 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388778 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388780 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388781 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388782 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388783 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388784 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388785 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388785 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388787 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388788 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388789 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388790 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388791 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388792 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388792 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(\\frac{4}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\frac{4}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388799 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{84}[\\begin{aligned}21 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{84}\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388801 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388802 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388803 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388804 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388805 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388806 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388806 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388808 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388809 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388810 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388811 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388812 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388813 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388813 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=24,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-24I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-24[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-24I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=24,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-24I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-24\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-24I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388815 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388816 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388817 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388818 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388819 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388820 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$" "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388883 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca7c2d5e2fe1729a3b076e63f9fe028ecb459b576f7843ab43e066a19ef89a81.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "![](images/ca/ca7c2d5e2fe1729a3b076e63f9fe028ecb459b576f7843ab43e066a19ef89a81.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388883 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-9,3)$, $(-9,3),$ $(3,-1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/84/84edf48b555f43cfb4866b9798b03110a609c88e92e83ad1fca17a926718a67e.png)" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-9,3)$, $(-9,3),$ $(3,-1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/84/84edf48b555f43cfb4866b9798b03110a609c88e92e83ad1fca17a926718a67e.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186388890 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d88ae8667df81624f0a28295c980f846ae88e9b4766fe0d0f692dd75509a56e9.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "![](images/d8/d88ae8667df81624f0a28295c980f846ae88e9b4766fe0d0f692dd75509a56e9.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388890 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(1,4)$, $(5,20)$, and  $(2,8).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/94/94247512fd446447144d808c76d00bc9c0d50d4175697597360117eca00b3cf1.png)" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(1,4)$, $(5,20)$, and  $(2,8).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/94/94247512fd446447144d808c76d00bc9c0d50d4175697597360117eca00b3cf1.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186388897 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "![](images/0c/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388897 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/c6/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/c6/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186388932 :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{|