trix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=2$ seconds." "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=2$ seconds."] [:db/cas 17592186389615 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-1+{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(2)}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2(0)}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3(0)} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-1+{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2(2)}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186389617 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4