n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\,1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $G=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G.$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nG=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}+2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\,1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $G=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G.$"] [:db/cas 17592186390492 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and the given matrix $G,$ we have\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $\\sqrt{3}$ on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+1}$ So, we get\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G & =P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}]\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{al