ing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b4/b406c864e820819c5ae6f548c7a025a1e1d8197fb785ea5689080034e04210cb.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391187 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391194 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/a2/a27e83035006f198d454498aafe91624e0576ab43993bde970faf426a9e4cef2.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/a2/a27e83035006f198d454498aafe91624e0576ab43993bde970faf426a9e4cef2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391219 :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)" "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391226 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0d/0d9e032bfffa68f487347d6c169999e313247e89ef7de3fae28edb216dcf718f.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0d/0d9e032bfffa68f487347d6c169999e313247e89ef7de3fae28edb216dcf718f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391257 :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-1$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92efcce675a436ad349ca6139cb12cda88fbdd5bdeaf2a5f1e755e7ba1f6d037.png)" "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-1$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92efcce675a436ad349ca6139cb12cda88fbdd5bdeaf2a5f1e755e7ba1f6d037.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391264 :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{1}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=A[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=AΦ(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$" "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{1}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}=A\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}=AΦ(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186391286 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/99/99629c2f73e3a10db2ef139848c93c6a2489d995d3d992b7dfb0332989b4f48f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/99/99629c2f73e3a10db2ef139848c93c6a2489d995d3d992b7dfb0332989b4f48f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391293 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b3/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/12/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b3/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/12/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391300 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186391300 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186391312 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field