pendent}$ on $R.$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186391324 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}}