ly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-2[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+0[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begi