owing curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-2 \\\\ y=2t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/54/54bdfaf14b4f580e1476d4649e03cd71cb8203e23ae318daa65251fd7ce02ba1.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=-2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/ed/edb5558ed7b5ee0e4aacb4eb67d74747fcc366f1508e16b3e99f3e0d5ee53306.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/10/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png)" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/60/6044d391016e37af929b36e89e0a802151b5710707cc510e9e7f70bf9a2d1c0d.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-2 \\\\ y=2t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/54/54bdfaf14b4f580e1476d4649e03cd71cb8203e23ae318daa65251fd7ce02ba1.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=-2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/ed/edb5558ed7b5ee0e4aacb4eb67d74747fcc366f1508e16b3e99f3e0d5ee53306.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/10/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391579 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186391579 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186391581 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186391582 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186391583 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186391584 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186391585 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186391586 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-1) \\\\ y(-1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned