n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}14-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(14-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )+192 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{10}$ | $+$ | $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{12}-4{x}^{10}+{x}^{3}-2x-3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{12}$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3$ |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,4x$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(2x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{10}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(2A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-3I) \\\\  & =2A-3I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}14 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -28\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}14-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(14-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )+192 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{10}$ | $+$ | $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- 