hose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392168 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392169 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5