d{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392356 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392357 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392358 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392359 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392360 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392361 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392361 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}17 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392363 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392364 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatr