{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={30}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392370 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -25 & 5 \\\\ -25 & 4 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -32 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -25 & 5 \\\\ -25 & 4 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -32 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392371 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392372 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 25 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 25 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 25 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 25 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392373 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392374 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392389 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186392389 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392391 :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" "R=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392392 :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392393 :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" "R=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392394 :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" "R=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392395 :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392402 :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186392402 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\text{ }\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392404 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392405 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392406 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392407 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{15}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392408 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\fr