-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,d\\,|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{16}{16}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,d\\,|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186392422 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset