$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392450 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392451 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186392452 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bm