nd ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{5t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5t{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(3t+1){e}^{3t} \\\\ (4t+1){e}^{4t} \\\\ (5t+1){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393107 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393108 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(t+3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (t+4){e}^{4t} \\\\ (t+5){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(t+3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (t+4){e}^{4t} \\\\ (t+5){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393109 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5t{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5t{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393110 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{3t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2){e}^{4t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+3){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{3t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2){e}^{4t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+3){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393111 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(3t+1){e}^{3t} \\\\ (4t+1){e}^{4t} \\\\ (5t+1){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(3t+1){e}^{3t} \\\\ (4t+1){e}^{4t} \\\\ (5t+1){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393112 :question/problem "Find ${(u(t)f(t))}^{′}$ where $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}.$" "Find ${(u(t)f(t))}^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393112 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)f(t))}^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-7)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{3}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-7){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)f(t))}^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-7)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{t}^{3}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-7){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393114 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(4{t}^{2}+t){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}-1){e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(4{t}^{2}+t){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}-1){e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393115 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393116 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(-5{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (3{t}^{2}-t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(-5{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (3{t}^{2}-t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393117 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}-4t+4) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-1) \\\\ (2t+3)\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}({t}^{2}-4t+4) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-1) \\\\ (2t+3)\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393118 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7{t}^{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}7{t}^{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393167 :question/problem "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $c$ onto $d.$" "Let $c=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $d=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $c$ onto $d.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393215 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393217 :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "x=-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393218 :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393219 :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393220 :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393221 :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393292 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=12.$" "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393292 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -18 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -18 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393294 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 18 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 18 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393295 :answer/value "12[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -18 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "12\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -18 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393296 :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "-\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393297 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393298 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393327 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $P$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $P$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393327 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $P$ has $2$ pivots in the $1$st and $2$nd columns:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $P$ has $2$ pivots in the $1$st and $2$nd columns:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column."] [:db/cas 17592186393334 :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393334 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393336 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393337 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393338 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393339 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393340 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393341 :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393341 